---
import PineLayout from "../layouts/PineLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/page/PageHero.astro";
import Subgrid from "@getvitops/astro/components/Subgrid.astro";

export const prerender = false;

// --- IT & HR Operations: priced per user, per month -------------------------
//
// "User", not "employee", everywhere on this page. It's the wider word — a
// contractor isn't an employee but still has accounts and still runs through
// payroll — and it's the unit we actually bill on, so it stays consistent with
// the back-office lines below.
// ONE plan, plus two add-ons. Three attempts at a tier ladder collapsed here
// (Basics/Foundations/Managed, then Foundations/Framework/Keystone), and the
// reason is structural rather than a naming problem: tiers work when
// capabilities cluster into bundles that map to buyer segments, and these
// don't. There is one operational service, plus two discrete things that scale
// differently from it — backup is a licence pass-through with a real per-user
// cost, response time is a labour commitment. Add-ons price both honestly;
// every tier draft had to explain the bundling away in an FAQ. If you find
// yourself drawing a ladder again, check whether the new tier is a capability
// cluster or just an add-on wearing a tier's clothes.
//
// MFA is in the BASE and stays there. Charging extra for multi-factor in 2026
// is charging for the lock on the door, and every competitor who tiers it looks
// worse the moment a buyer notices. It's also the only part of the old "access
// security" line we can actually deliver.
//
// SSO is deliberately NOT sold. Most SaaS vendors gate SAML behind their own
// enterprise plan (the "SSO tax"), so the client pays per-app upgrades to enable
// the very thing we'd charge to configure — billing for access to a cost we just
// imposed on them. Revisit only if we standardise on an IDaaS (JumpCloud is the
// SMB-priced one; Okta's SKUs stack past what this segment absorbs), and even
// then it's a pass-through, not margin.
//
// Managed EDR/MDM and device management are NOT offered — see the honest-gap
// callout below the plan, which stands in for the pulled "Keystone" tier.
// Identity provisioning IS: the HRIS integration drives licence and access
// changes off the employee record. How far that reaches depends on what each
// app supports, which is why the copy says "driven by your HRIS" rather than
// promising zero-touch across an arbitrary stack.
//
// BASE + PER-SEAT, not flat per-user. Most of this work is fixed regardless of
// headcount — stack selection, the HRIS, keeping integrations alive, the
// monthly report — while only provisioning and support scale with people. A
// pure per-user rate therefore mispriced both ends: it under-recovered on a
// 10-person client and over-charged a 50-person one for the same fixed effort.
// $450 + $29 carries the fixed half explicitly and lets the effective per-user
// rate fall as the team grows ($74 at 10 users, $38 at 50), which is the shape
// the example table makes visible rather than leaving a reader to compute.
//
// The HRIS sits here rather than in back office because JML runs against it; a
// system of record priced in a different pillar from the workflow that depends
// on it was a dependency sold away from its dependant.
const softwarePlan = {
	name: "Business software operations",
	price: "$450",
	unit: "/ mo + $29 per user",
	setup: "from $1,500 setup · waived with annual commitment",
	blurb:
		"The whole software side of the business, run by one team — chosen, wired, kept current, and answered for.",
	features: [
		"Vendor-neutral software selection and roadmap",
		"Onboarding, offboarding, and role changes (JML), driven by your HRIS — licence and access changes follow the record",
		"Your HRIS configured and maintained",
		"Multi-factor authentication on every account, enforced and verified",
		"Integrations maintained, plus 4 hours/month of changes and small integration work included — larger implementations are scoped as fixed-price projects before we start",
		"Email & ticket support in business hours, first response by next business day",
		"Monthly ops report: licence utilization and savings found, MFA compliance status, joiner/leaver log",
	],
};

// Example totals, rendered as a table so a reader sees the curve rather than
// doing the arithmetic. Kept as data so the base/seat rates above and these
// figures can't drift apart silently — recompute on any price change.
const planExamples = [
	{ users: 10, monthly: "$740", perUser: "$74" },
	{ users: 25, monthly: "$1,175", perUser: "$47" },
	{ users: 50, monthly: "$1,900", perUser: "$38" },
];

// Add-ons state ABSOLUTE commitments, never relative ones. "Priority support —
// ahead of the queue" was drafted and cut: priority over whom? It means less
// with every client who buys it, and the ones who bought first are the ones who
// notice. A stated response time stays true at any volume.
//
// ⚠️ Managed backup is not confirmed as deliverable — the tooling has to exist
// before this ships. Wholesale runs ~$3–5.50 CAD/user/mo (Veeam, Dropsuite,
// Datto, Keepit), so $8 still clears the licence with margin for the labour.
//
// ⚠️ "Canada-resident storage by default" is a NEW delivery commitment on top of
// that: it rules out any vendor without a Canadian region, so confirm the
// chosen tool actually offers one before this line goes live.
//
// Note this is the ONE data-residency promise on the page that actually ships.
// Sovereign (website block) is unbuilt and unpriced. The two aren't in conflict
// — backup storage is a vendor setting, sovereign hosting is a different
// application stack — but if this line can't be honoured, drop it rather than
// leaving the page with no residency claim it can keep.
const softwareAddons = [
	{
		name: "Managed backup",
		price: "$8",
		period: "/ user / mo",
		included: [
			"Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace backup (mail, files, chat)",
			"Canada-resident backup storage by default",
			"Controlled retention window and restore on request",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Priority response",
		price: "$10",
		period: "/ user / mo",
		included: [
			"First response within one business hour, against next business day on the base plan",
			"An escalation path agreed up front, with a named person at the end of it",
		],
	},
];

// --- Digital Presence: priced per service -----------------------------------
const presenceServices = [
	{
		name: "SEO & GEO optimization",
		price: "$650",
		period: "/ mo",
		included: [
			"Ongoing search + AI-answer-engine optimization with monthly reporting",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Ad campaign management",
		price: "$500",
		period: "/ mo + spend",
		included: [
			"Curated Google / social campaigns, steered monthly for conversions",
			"Management fee only — ad spend billed separately, no markup",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Social media management",
		price: "$650",
		period: "/ mo",
		included: [
			"Content calendar and posting across up to 3 platforms, kept on brand",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Business listings",
		price: "$75",
		period: "/ mo",
		included: [
			"Google Business Profile, Bing Places, Apple Business Connect — claimed, verified, kept in sync",
		],
	},
];

// --- Back Office ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Bookkeeping's rate is confirmed. Payroll, benefits administration and HR
// operations are still ⚠️ PLACEHOLDER figures — plausible Canadian SMB numbers
// chosen so the structure could be reviewed. Confirm them before production.
//
// Payroll $30 and benefits $20 PEPM are decided, not derived — both sit at the
// service-level comparables in the market benchmark (payroll's bureau row, not
// its software row), so the per-user line is the all-in price of serving one
// person. No base fee sits on top of any of the three: at an all-in rate a base
// double-counts the same work. A monthly minimum carries the small end instead,
// which is how these are actually sold — the benchmark notes minimums bind.
//
// The HRIS is no longer a back-office line. It sits in the business software
// plan instead — it's the system of record joiner/mover/leaver runs against,
// and JML is in that plan too, so splitting the two across pillars priced a
// dependency away from the thing that depends on it. Nothing on this page
// charges separately for it now; it's inside the per-user plan price. Don't
// reintroduce an "HR operations" line without deciding what it contains beyond
// the HRIS.
//
// Both $150 minimums are CONFIRMED (2026-08-01). They aren't benchmarked — the
// source PEPM table has no minimum or base column — they're a decided floor.
// At $30 payroll that's ~5 users and at $20 benefits ~8, which is where the
// fixed per-client work (the pay-run calendar, the enrolment setup) stops being
// covered by headcount alone. They're the reason the small end doesn't run at a
// loss, so don't drop them to win a five-person client.
//
// Bookkeeping is the one genuine exception and stays banded: it scales with
// transaction volume and entity count, so a 12-person contractor with three
// entities and heavy AP is more work than a 40-person firm with clean books,
// and $/user misprices both ends. Do NOT reintroduce a blanket "priced per
// service, not per seat" claim — three of the four lines now disprove it.
//
// Joiner/mover/leaver is intentionally absent — it spans both halves and is
// already priced inside the business software plan. Don't double-charge it.
const backOfficeServices = [
	{
		name: "Bookkeeping",
		price: "$300–800",
		period: "/ mo",
		included: [
			"Transaction coding, bank and credit card reconciliations, and a monthly close — the books kept current rather than rebuilt at year-end",
			"Banded by transaction volume and number of entities — we quote after a look at your last quarter",
			"Accounts payable and receivable, job costing and inventory tracking are priced on top of the band",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Managed payroll",
		price: "$30",
		period: "/ user / mo · $150 minimum",
		included: [
			"Pay runs, direct deposits, CRA remittances, T4s and ROEs, run to schedule",
			"Year-end filing and audit-ready documentation",
		],
	},
	{
		name: "Benefits administration",
		price: "$20",
		period: "/ user / mo · $150 minimum",
		included: [
			"Enrolments, life events, terminations, and payroll deduction sync",
			"Employee questions handled directly, so they don't come to you",
			"Your broker keeps the plan — we run the daily administration of it",
		],
	},
];

// --- Website plans: three mutually exclusive tiers, pick one -----------------
const hostingTiers = [
	{
		name: "Essential",
		price: "$39",
		unit: "/ mo",
		annual: "$34/mo annual",
		setup: "$750 setup",
		features: [
			"AI-built Astro site with security updates, analytics, and transactional email",
			"2 small edits / mo",
			"Everything competitors nickel-and-dime, included: uptime monitoring, SSL, backups, staging environment",
			"Ships SEO- & AI-search (GEO) optimized at build: Core Web Vitals, structured data, sitemaps, llms.txt, FAQ schema",
		],
		highlighted: false,
	},
	{
		name: "Signal",
		price: "$89",
		unit: "/ mo",
		annual: "$79/mo annual",
		setup: "$750 setup",
		tag: "Most popular",
		features: [
			"Everything in Essential, plus:",
			"Server-side conversion tracking (Meta CAPI, Google enhanced conversions)",
			"Qualified-lead alerts — a text, email, or call when a lead matches your rules, not every form fire",
			"Automated monthly SEO & performance report: keyword ranks, Search Console, Core Web Vitals, conversion summary",
			"4 edits / mo with priority turnaround",
		],
		highlighted: true,
	},
	{
		name: "Growth",
		price: "$229",
		unit: "/ mo",
		annual: "$199/mo annual",
		setup: "$750 setup, +$500 tracking / rules config",
		features: [
			"Everything in Signal, plus the managed layer:",
			"Monthly A/B test cycle with plain-language results",
			"Quarterly 30-minute strategy call",
			"One landing page per quarter",
			"6 edits / mo",
			"Best above ~500 sessions / mo — below that, heatmap-driven iteration instead. No ads management or e-commerce.",
		],
		highlighted: false,
	},
];

const finePrint = [
	"No long-term contracts — month-to-month by default, with an optional annual commitment that waives setup",
	"Our fees are for the work, not the software — you keep your own vendor licences at your own price, and we'll tell you when a feature needs a licence tier you don't have yet",
	"No hidden fees — ad spend and one-time project costs are always itemized separately",
	"Every plan starts with a conversation, not a form into a void",
];
---

<PineLayout
	title="Pricing"
	description="Three services, priced simply. Software operations at a flat base plus a per-user rate, HRIS included; bookkeeping banded by volume, payroll and benefits administration per user; managed website plans from $39/mo. Month-to-month.">
	<!-- PAGE INTRO -->
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="Pricing"
		title="Three services, priced simply."
		lead="Month-to-month by default — cancel with 30 days' notice. Business software carries a one-time setup, waived on an annual commitment; website plans include a one-time build & setup. These prices are a starting point for planning; your actual quote depends on team size and scope."
		secondaryLabel="See what funding covers"
		secondaryHref="#pricing-funding"
	/>

	<!-- BUSINESS SOFTWARE -->
	<section class="section centered" id="it-hr-pricing">
		<div class="spotlight">
			<header class="section-intro">
				<span class="font-eyebrow">Business software</span>
				<h2 class="font-title">
					A flat base plus a small per-seat rate — so the price fits your size.
				</h2>
				<p class="font-body section-lead">
					The software your business runs on: selection, integration, the HRIS,
					joiners and leavers, multi-factor authentication, and support. One plan
					rather than a ladder — we'd rather sell one thing that means something
					than three that need a chart. A user is anyone who needs accounts and
					access — employees and contractors alike — so you're billed for the
					people you actually set up, not your headcount. Bookkeeping, payroll and
					benefits administration are
					<a class="link" href="#back-office-pricing">priced separately</a>.
				</p>
			</header>

			{/*
				Stated before the price, deliberately. "We don't resell software" is
				the thing that makes the fee legible — without it a reader assumes a
				margin on licences and reads the base fee as a second charge for the
				same thing. It also sets up the licence pass-through note further
				down, which would otherwise look like a reseller hedge.
			*/}
			<p class="font-lead neutrality-note">
				<strong>We don't resell software.</strong> Your licences stay yours, at
				your price. Our fee is for making them work — which means we make money
				when your software bill goes down, not up.
			</p>

			{/*
				One plan, so no Subgrid here — a subgrid exists to align tranches
				across sibling cards, and there are no siblings. The website block
				below still uses it for its three tiers.
			*/}
			<article class="card plan-solo pricing-tier--featured">
				<div class="plan-head">
					<div class="plan-identity">
						<h3 class="font-heading tier-name">{softwarePlan.name}</h3>
						<p class="font-body tier-blurb">{softwarePlan.blurb}</p>
					</div>
					<div class="plan-price-block">
						<p class="tier-price">
							<span class="tier-price__amount">{softwarePlan.price}</span>
							<span class="tier-price__unit">{softwarePlan.unit}</span>
						</p>
						<p class="tier-sub">{softwarePlan.setup}</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<ul class="feature-list plan-features">
					{softwarePlan.features.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
				</ul>

				{/*
					Tabular data, so a real <table>. Wrapped in its own overflow-x
					container: wide content scrolls inside itself, never the page body.
					The point it makes is the curve — the effective per-user rate falls
					as the team grows — which a reader shouldn't have to compute.
				*/}
				<div class="plan-examples">
					<table>
						<caption class="font-caption">
							Example totals at $450/mo + $29 per user.
						</caption>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th scope="col">Team size</th>
								<th scope="col">Monthly</th>
								<th scope="col">Per user</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							{
								planExamples.map((row) => (
									<tr>
										<th scope="row">{row.users}</th>
										<td>{row.monthly}</td>
										<td>{row.perUser}</td>
									</tr>
								))
							}
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>

				<a class="cta plan-cta" href="/#contact">
					Get started <span class="arrow">→</span>
				</a>
			</article>

			<h3 class="font-heading addons-head">Add-ons</h3>
			<div class="svc-table card">
				{
					softwareAddons.map((svc) => (
						<div class="svc-row">
							<div class="svc-head">
								<h4 class="font-heading svc-name">{svc.name}</h4>
								<p class="svc-price">
									<span class="svc-price__amount">{svc.price}</span>
									<span class="svc-price__period">{svc.period}</span>
								</p>
							</div>
							<ul class="svc-included">
								{svc.included.map((line) => (
									<li>{line}</li>
								))}
							</ul>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>

			{/*
				The reseller line is deliberately conditional. We can't promise a
				margin we don't control — distributor pricing moves, and not every
				vendor in a given stack is available to us at all — so it says "where
				we can" and tells the reader to assume list until we've checked. Don't
				harden it into a stated discount without a rate we can actually hold.
			*/}
			<p class="section-note breakout">
				These are our fees, on top of the software licences themselves — you buy
				those from the vendor at the vendor's price. Where we can supply a
				licence through our distribution partners for less than list, we pass
				that price through; assume list until we've checked your particular
				stack. Some features need a particular licence tier before we can switch
				them on, and we'll tell you which on the first call rather than after
				you've signed. Running something this plan doesn't cover? Ask us about
				our other managed services.
			</p>

			{/*
				The honest-gap note, in place of the pulled top tier. Managed detection and
				response isn't something we run yet, so it isn't on the page — not
				greyed out, not "coming soon". A disabled price card still occupies
				the comparison row and still sets an expectation, and the reader who
				wants MDR is the strongest lead here; the first thing they'd learn
				would be that it isn't real. Saying it plainly is the same move the
				rest of the site makes ("we're the wrong shop", "we'll point you at
				it"), and it costs less credibility than a tier we can't sell.
			*/}
			<div class="callout-solo">
				<article class="card callout">
					<span class="font-eyebrow callout-eyebrow callout-eyebrow--muted">
						What we don't run yet
					</span>
					<h3 class="font-heading callout-title">
						Managed detection and response isn't ours to promise.
					</h3>
					<p class="font-body callout-copy">
						Endpoint detection and response, and device management, aren't things
						we operate today. If that's what your insurer, your client or your
						own risk appetite calls for right now, we'll tell you on the first
						call and point you at someone who does it properly — rather than sell
						you a plan and work it out afterwards.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- BACK OFFICE -->
	<section class="section centered" id="back-office-pricing">
		<div class="spotlight">
			<header class="section-intro">
				<span class="font-eyebrow">Back office</span>
				<h2 class="font-title">Take some or take all of them.</h2>
				<p class="font-lead section-lead">
					The books, the pay runs, the benefits changes and the employee records
					— run to schedule by the same partner that runs your systems.
					Payroll and benefits administration are priced per user, because every
					pay slip and every enrolment genuinely is one more person. Bookkeeping
					is the exception — it's banded by transaction volume and entity count,
					since that's what actually drives the work.
					Take one, take all of them, or combine them with the other two
					services for{" "}
					<a class="link" href="#pricing-bundles">a bundle discount</a>.
				</p>
			</header>

			<div class="svc-table card">
				{
					backOfficeServices.map((svc) => (
						<div class="svc-row">
							<div class="svc-head">
								<h3 class="font-heading svc-name">{svc.name}</h3>
								<p class="svc-price">
									<span class="svc-price__amount">{svc.price}</span>
									<span class="svc-price__period">{svc.period}</span>
								</p>
							</div>
							<ul class="svc-included">
								{svc.included.map((line) => (
									<li>{line}</li>
								))}
							</ul>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>

			<p class="section-note breakout">
				Onboarding and offboarding isn't listed here — it spans both halves and
				is already in every{" "}
				<a class="link" href="#it-hr-pricing">business software</a> plan.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- DIGITAL PRESENCE -->
	<section class="section centered" id="digital-presence-pricing">
		<div class="spotlight">
			<header class="section-intro">
				<span class="font-eyebrow">Digital Presence</span>
				<h2 class="font-title">One managed website plan. Pick your tier.</h2>
				<p class="font-lead section-lead">
					Build, hosting, and care in a single monthly price — pick one tier,
					add services as you grow. Annual billing saves roughly two months.
				</p>
			</header>

			<Subgrid class="pricing-tiers">
				{
					hostingTiers.map((tier) => (
						<article
							class:list={[
								"card",
								"pricing-tier",
								tier.highlighted ? "pricing-tier--featured" : "",
							]}>
							<div class="tier-head">
								<div class="tier-name-row">
									<h3 class="font-heading tier-name">{tier.name}</h3>
									{tier.tag && <span class="tier-tag">{tier.tag}</span>}
								</div>
								<p class="tier-price">
									<span class="tier-price__amount">{tier.price}</span>
									<span class="tier-price__unit">{tier.unit}</span>
								</p>
								<p class="tier-sub">
									{tier.annual} · {tier.setup}
								</p>
							</div>
							<ul class="feature-list">
								{tier.features.map((f) => (
									<li>{f}</li>
								))}
							</ul>
							<a
								class:list={["tier-cta", tier.highlighted ? "cta" : "cta-ghost"]}
								href="/#contact">
								Get started <span class="arrow">→</span>
							</a>
						</article>
					))
				}
			</Subgrid>

			{/*
				Deliberately UNPRICED. This is demand research, not a product: the
				site plans run on the Cloudflare Workers adapter with EmDash bound to
				D1, R2, KV and Images, so "Canadian-owned hosting" is a port of the
				application stack, not a setting — there's no build target for it
				today. The old "+$39/mo · any tier" implied a toggle and wouldn't
				have covered the hard cost (a Canadian VPS plus Matomo runs $65–110
				CAD/mo per client, and it doesn't amortise the way Workers does).

				A price makes a reader evaluate and move on; no price makes them
				raise their hand, which is the signal we actually want while we work
				out whether enough people need this. Inbound mentions ARE the data —
				if this stays quiet, that's the answer. Don't put a number back until
				there's a build target and a per-client cost we can hold.
			*/}
			<article class="card sovereign">
				<div class="sovereign-head">
					<span class="font-eyebrow callout-eyebrow">Sovereign</span>
					<span class="sovereign-status">In the works — not available yet</span>
				</div>
				<p class="sovereign-copy">
					Canadian-owned hosting for your site, with no US-controlled services
					in the stack around it — Matomo rather than Google Analytics, and
					backups held in Canada. We're actively looking into it. Being honest
					about where it stands: hosting somewhere Canadian-owned is a different
					stack rather than a setting, so it's real work and we'd rather scope
					it properly than half-ship it.
				</p>
				<p class="sovereign-copy">
					If data sovereignty is a genuine constraint for you rather than a
					preference — a regulator, a client contract, a board that asks —
					<a class="link" href="/#contact">tell us</a>. Who asks, and why, is
					what decides whether we build it and how soon.
				</p>
			</article>

			<p class="section-note breakout">
				Extra work beyond your plan's included edits is billed at $80 / hour.
			</p>

			<h3 class="font-heading addons-head">Add-on services</h3>
			<div class="svc-table card">
				{
					presenceServices.map((svc) => (
						<div class="svc-row">
							<div class="svc-head">
								<h3 class="font-heading svc-name">{svc.name}</h3>
								<p class="svc-price">
									<span class="svc-price__amount">{svc.price}</span>
									<span class="svc-price__period">{svc.period}</span>
								</p>
							</div>
							<ul class="svc-included">
								{svc.included.map((line) => (
									<li>{line}</li>
								))}
							</ul>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</div>
